<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ermengol VI of Urgell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1096">1096</a>–<a href="/years/1154">1154</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ermengol VI of Urgell (1096–1154), called 'of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>', was Count of Urgell in Catalonia from 1102. Raised partly in Castile, he served the Castilian kings and held lands there, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, while extending Urgell southwards against the Muslims. In 1149 he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</a> in capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>, which they shared.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ermengol-vi-of-urgell.jpg" alt="Ermengol VI of Urgell" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nekto; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler work: Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Comtats_catalans_1035-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ermengol VI was born in Valladolid in 1096, the son and successor of Count Ermengol V of Urgell and María Pérez, daughter of Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ansurez">Pedro Ansúrez</a>, Lord of Valladolid. His birthplace gave him the nickname el de Castilla, the one from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Orphaned in 1102, he succeeded as a child under the regency of his grandfather Pedro Ansúrez, who also became his tutor, though real power lay with Guerau II of Cabrera and Raymond Berenguer III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. With their help the young count took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a> in 1105 and made it his capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ermengol divided his service between Urgell and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. He fought beside Alfonso the Battler at the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1118 and later served Alfonso VII the Emperor as mayordomo mayor. In 1146 he was one of the commanders of Alfonso's army at the battle of Albacete, and in 1147 he joined the emperor's expedition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>. In 1133 he ceded Andorra to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Urgell.</p><p>He kept good relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-barcelona">House of Barcelona</a> and accompanied Raymond Berenguer IV to Provence in 1144. In 1149 he helped Raymond Berenguer in the conquest and repopulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>, receiving one-third of the city, which thereafter formed part of his dominion. He was also a patron of religious houses, among them Solsona Cathedral and the Monastery of Santa María de Retuerta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ansurez">Pedro Ansúrez</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Before August 1126 Ermengol married Arsenda de Cabrera, daughter of Guerau II de Cabrera, first Viscount of Àger; the couple later divorced. Their son Ermengol VII succeeded him as Count of Urgell. Before September 1135 he married Elvira Rodríguez, daughter of Count Rodrigo González de Lara and Sancha of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, a daughter of King Alfonso VI of León. Their daughters were María, known as Maria de Almenara, wife of Lope López de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Vizcaya</a>, and Isabel, whose marriage to Ramon Folc III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardona">Cardona</a> was negotiated in 1152.</p><p>On 24 March 1144 Ermengol drew up his will, naming among his executors his wife Elvira and his father-in-law, Rodrigo González de Lara. He asked to be buried in Solsona Cathedral, but when he died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile</a> on 20 June 1154 he was buried instead at the Monastery of Santa María de Valbuena, founded by his sister Stephanie.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ermengol VI” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1149 Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona and <b>Ermengol VI of Urgell</b> captured the city, adding it to what became the Principality of Catalonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-lerida">Taifa of Lérida</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in the Kingdom of Castile. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-4.73&amp;year=1133" aria-label="Valladolid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4435 680 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ermengol VI of Urgell"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4885 950h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4885 950h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.65,-4.73&amp;year=1133">What was here in 1133? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
